.TH NP_PACKET 3 "June 2026" "netpipe v0.1.0" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
np_packet_alloc, np_packet_free, np_packet_clone, np_packet_ts_str,
np_packet_t, np_layer_t, np_proto_t, np_http_msg_t, np_dns_msg_t,
np_str_t, np_linktype_t
\- netpipe packet structures and lifecycle
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <netpipe.h>
.PP
.BI "np_packet_t *np_packet_alloc(size_t " caplen ");"
.br
.BI "void         np_packet_free(np_packet_t *" pkt ");"
.br
.BI "np_packet_t *np_packet_clone(const np_packet_t *" src ");"
.br
.BI "void         np_packet_ts_str(const np_packet_t *" pkt ", char *" buf ", size_t " bufsz ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions and structures represent and manage individual network packets
in the netpipe library.
.SS Packet Lifecycle
.PP
The
.BR np_packet_alloc ()
function allocates a new packet structure with space for raw captured packet
bytes of up to
.I caplen
bytes.
It returns a pointer to the newly allocated packet or
.B NULL
on failure.
.PP
The
.BR np_packet_free ()
function releases all memory associated with the packet, including any
dynamically allocated
.B stream_data
buffer.
Passing
.B NULL
is safe.
.PP
The
.BR np_packet_clone ()
function creates a complete deep copy of
.IR src ,
including
.BR raw ,
.BR layers[] ,
.BR stream_data ,
and the scratch region.
Returns
.B NULL
on failure.
.PP
The
.BR np_packet_ts_str ()
function formats the packet's capture timestamp into
.IR buf
as the string
.BR "HH:MM:SS.uuuuuu" .
.I bufsz
must be at least 16; 32 is recommended to be safe.
.SS Structures
.PP
A packet is represented by the
.B np_packet_t
structure:
.PP
.nf
typedef struct np_packet {
    struct timespec  ts;          /* capture timestamp         */
    uint32_t         caplen;      /* bytes captured            */
    uint32_t         wirelen;     /* bytes on the wire         */
    uint8_t         *raw;         /* raw frame buffer          */

    int              nlayers;
    np_layer_t       layers[8];   /* decoded layer stack       */

    const np_layer_t *eth;        /* link layer (or NULL)      */
    const np_layer_t *net;        /* network layer (or NULL)   */
    const np_layer_t *transport;  /* transport layer (or NULL) */
    const np_layer_t *app;        /* app layer (or NULL)       */

    uint64_t         seq;         /* capture sequence number   */
    uint32_t         flow_id;     /* 5-tuple flow hash         */

    uint8_t         *stream_data; /* reassembled TCP payload   */
    size_t           stream_len;

    void            *user_data;   /* caller-owned context      */

    /* internal — do not access directly */
    uint8_t          scratch[8192];
    size_t           scratch_used;

    void            *reserved[4]; /* must be NULL              */
} np_packet_t;
.fi
.PP
Each protocol layer is described by
.BR np_layer_t :
.PP
.nf
typedef struct np_layer {
    np_proto_t     proto;   /* protocol identifier                    */
    const uint8_t *data;    /* pointer into raw[] — not copied        */
    size_t         len;     /* bytes in this layer (hdr + payload)    */
    void          *decoded; /* points into scratch (or NULL)          */
} np_layer_t;
.fi
.PP
The
.B decoded
pointer, when non-NULL, points to a protocol-specific structure in
the packet's scratch region (see
.B Decoded Protocol Objects
below).
It is valid only for the lifetime of the
.B np_packet_t
it belongs to.
.SS Protocol Identifiers
.PP
.B np_proto_t
is the enum of protocols that the built-in demuxer can decode:
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_RAW
Raw IP frame (no link header).
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_ETH
Ethernet (IEEE 802.3).
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_ARP
Address Resolution Protocol.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_IP4
Internet Protocol version 4.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_IP6
Internet Protocol version 6.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_ICMP
ICMP or ICMPv6.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_TCP
Transmission Control Protocol.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_UDP
User Datagram Protocol.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_DNS
Domain Name System (application layer).
The layer's
.B decoded
pointer, if non-NULL, points to an
.B np_dns_msg_t
structure.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_HTTP
HTTP/1.x (application layer).
The layer's
.B decoded
pointer, if non-NULL, points to an
.B np_http_msg_t
structure.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_TLS
TLS/SSL (application layer).
No structured decode is currently performed; the raw bytes are accessible
via the layer's
.B data
and
.B len
fields.
.SS Zero-Copy String (np_str_t)
.PP
String fields inside decoded protocol structures use
.BR np_str_t ,
a non-owning slice that points directly into the packet's raw buffer:
.PP
.nf
typedef struct {
    const char *str;  /* NOT NUL-terminated */
    size_t      len;
} np_str_t;
.fi
.PP
Always use
.B len
to bound reads.
Do not rely on a NUL terminator.
The pointer is valid only for the lifetime of the enclosing
.BR np_packet_t .
.SS Decoded Protocol Objects
.PP
The decoded HTTP message is represented by
.BR np_http_msg_t .
Access it via
.B pkt->app->decoded
when
.B pkt->app->proto == NP_PROTO_HTTP
and
.B pkt->app->decoded != NULL\fR:
.PP
.nf
typedef struct {
    bool     is_request;      /* true=request, false=response  */

    np_str_t method;          /* "GET", "POST", etc.           */
    np_str_t path;            /* request URI                   */
    np_str_t version;         /* "HTTP/1.1"                    */

    int      status_code;     /* response: 200, 404, …         */
    np_str_t status_phrase;   /* response: "OK", "Not Found"   */

    int              num_headers;
    np_http_header_t headers[32];

    const uint8_t   *body;
    size_t           body_len;
} np_http_msg_t;
.fi
.PP
The decoded DNS message is represented by
.BR np_dns_msg_t .
Access it via
.B pkt->app->decoded
when
.B pkt->app->proto == NP_PROTO_DNS
and
.B pkt->app->decoded != NULL\fR:
.PP
.nf
typedef struct {
    uint16_t id;              /* transaction ID               */
    bool     is_response;     /* true=response, false=query   */
    int      rcode;           /* 0=NOERROR, 3=NXDOMAIN, …    */

    char     query_name[256]; /* queried domain (NUL-term.)   */
    uint16_t query_type;      /* A=1, AAAA=28, CNAME=5, …     */

    int             num_answers;
    np_dns_answer_t answers[8];
} np_dns_msg_t;
.fi
.PP
Each
.B np_dns_answer_t
contains:
.PP
.nf
typedef struct {
    char     name[256];      /* owner name (NUL-terminated)  */
    uint16_t type;           /* record type                  */
    uint16_t class_;         /* class (1=IN)                 */
    uint32_t ttl;            /* time-to-live in seconds      */
    uint16_t data_len;       /* wire length of RDATA         */
    char     rdata_str[256]; /* human-readable RDATA         */
} np_dns_answer_t;
.fi
.SS User Data
.PP
The
.B user_data
field of
.B np_packet_t
is never read or written by the library.
Callers may use it freely to attach per-packet context.
It is shallow-copied by
.BR np_packet_clone ().
.SH RETURN VALUES
.BR np_packet_alloc ()
and
.BR np_packet_clone ()
return a pointer to the allocated packet on success, or
.B NULL
on failure (memory exhaustion).
.SH NOTES
The
.B scratch[]
region and
.B scratch_used
counter are managed internally by the demuxer.
Do not read or write them directly; they are exposed in the struct only to
avoid a second heap allocation per packet.
The layout of
.B scratch[]
is not part of the stable ABI.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libnetpipe (3),
.BR np_pipeline (3)
